//! # Modules, packages, and native boundaries
//!
//! A Hew source file is a module. Import dotted module paths with `import` and
//! refer to the module through its final component. Standard-library imports
//! use names such as `std.math` and `std.encoding.json`.
//!
//! ```hew
//! import std.math;
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     println(math.max(3, 7));
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `import app.model;` imports a package module. A quoted import such as
//! `import "helpers.hew";` loads a source file relative to the importer. Add
//! `as name` to a whole-module import to choose its local qualifier, or use a
//! brace selection to import named public items.
//!
//! ## Visibility
//!
//! A declaration without a visibility keyword is private to its module.
//! `package` exposes it to the current package, and `pub` exposes it to importers.
//! The same keywords apply to functions, types, constants, actors, traits, and
//! other exported declarations. Imports form a directed acyclic graph; the
//! compiler reports the complete path when it finds a cycle.
//!
//! `hew init name` creates a package with `hew.toml`. `hew check`, `hew build`,
//! and `hew run` search parent directories for that manifest. The optional
//! `[package] main` field selects the entry file; otherwise it is `main.hew`.
//! Directories form nested namespaces. A directory module uses its matching
//! `name/name.hew` root and can be composed with peer files in that directory;
//! import paths still use dots rather than filesystem separators.
//!
//! ## Foreign functions and unsafe code
//!
//! `extern` blocks declare native symbols and ABI types. Calls that rely on raw
//! pointers or an unchecked host contract belong inside `unsafe { ... }`.
//! Package `[native]` configuration supplies the Rust, C, C++, or system-library
//! implementation linked into native builds. These declarations describe a
//! native boundary and do not imply WASI or browser availability; consult
//! [target capabilities](language.targets.html) before exposing them in a
//! portable package.
//!
//! ## Declarative macros
//!
//! `macro` declarations transform token trees during compilation. A macro call
//! uses `name!(...)`, `name![...]`, or `name!{...}` and expands before name and
//! type checking. Expansion is hygienic, bounded, and cannot perform arbitrary
//! build-time I/O. Use ordinary generic functions or traits when token-level
//! syntax generation is unnecessary.
